This is one line of CSS that[Style Dictionary](https://styledictionary.com/) wrote for us on 26 October 2026:


```text
--color-action-primary  :    var  (  --color-blue-600  )  ;    /** Background of the one primary action on a screen. Never use for links. */


```


A developer reads it and knows what to do. An AI coding agent reading the same line has to follow the` var()` to another line to get the colour, then parse a comment to find the rule. Both are possible. Neither is what the file was built for.


Style Dictionary can build a second file from the same tokens, shaped for the agent instead. This post shows the whole thing: the token file, the config, the command and the real output, run with the current release.


## What Style Dictionary does


**Style Dictionary** is an open-source build system that turns one set of design tokens into the files each platform needs: CSS variables, JavaScript modules, iOS and Android resources, and anything else you can write a format for. Its[GitHub repository](https://github.com/style-dictionary/style-dictionary) describes it as "a build system for creating cross-platform styles."


It started at Amazon. The[AWS open source blog](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/opensource/style-dictionary-trust-design-consistency/) says it was released on GitHub in summer 2017. It's widely used:[8,182,425 npm downloads](https://api.npmjs.org/downloads/point/last-month/style-dictionary) between 23 September and 22 October 2026.


We used **version 5.7.1** , published to[npm](https://www.npmjs.com/package/style-dictionary) on 24 October 2026, on Node 26.


## Step 1: write the tokens in DTCG format


The[Design Tokens Community Group](https://www.designtokens.org/) format uses` $value` ,` $type` and` $description` keys, and curly-brace aliases to point one token at another.[Style Dictionary's DTCG page](https://styledictionary.com/info/dtcg/) says it has had first-class support for the format since version 4. If you're deciding whether to move to DTCG at all, our look at[what 15 design systems actually ship](https://www.supernova.io/blog/dtcg-design-tokens-adoption) covers the state of adoption.


Our file,` tokens/tokens.json` , is deliberately small: two primitive colours, two semantic colours that alias them, and two spacing values.


```text
{
"color"  :    {
"$type"  :    "color"  ,
"blue"  :    {
"600"  :    {    "$value"  :    "#2563eb"    }
}  ,
"red"  :    {
"600"  :    {    "$value"  :    "#dc2626"    }
}  ,
"action"  :    {
"primary"  :    {
"$value"  :    "{color.blue.600}"  ,
"$description"  :    "Background of the one primary action on a screen. Never use for links."
}  ,
"destructive"  :    {
"$value"  :    "{color.red.600}"  ,
"$description"  :    "Background of actions that delete data. Pair with a confirm step."
}
}
}  ,
"spacing"  :    {
"$type"  :    "dimension"  ,
"200"  :    {
"$value"  :    {    "value"  :    8  ,    "unit"  :    "px"    }  ,
"$description"  :    "Gap between an icon and its label."
}  ,
"400"  :    {
"$value"  :    {    "value"  :    16  ,    "unit"  :    "px"    }  ,
"$description"  :    "Padding inside buttons and inputs."
}
}
}


```


Two details matter here:


- **` $type` is set once per group:** every token under` color` inherits` color` , and every token under` spacing` inherits` dimension` .
- **Dimensions are objects:**` { "value": 8, "unit": "px" }` is the shape in the stable 2025.10 spec. Style Dictionary's docs still say 2025.10 support is a work in progress, but 5.7.1 turned these into` 8px` and` 16px` without complaint in our run.


## Step 2: configure two platforms


A Style Dictionary **platform** is one output target with its own transforms and formats. We set up two:` css` for the browser, and` agent` for a file an agent can query.


The CSS platform uses only built-in pieces. The agent platform needs a custom format, which is the short function at the top of` config.mjs` :


```text
import    StyleDictionary     from    'style-dictionary'  ;


// One resolved entry per token, for agents to look up by name.
StyleDictionary  .  registerFormat  (  {
name  :    'json/agent'  ,
format  :    (  {   dictionary  }   )    =>    {
const   tokens  =   dictionary .  allTokens  .  map  (  (  token  )    =>    (  {
name  :   token .  path  .  join  (  '.'  )  ,
cssVar  :    `  --  ${  token .  name  }   `   ,
type  :   token .  $type  ,
value  :   token .  $value  ,
aliasOf  :    typeof   token .  original  .  $value    ===    'string'    &&   token .  original  .  $value  .  startsWith  (  '{'  )
?   token .  original  .  $value  .  slice  (  1  ,    -  1  )
:    null  ,
description  :   token .  $description    ??    null  ,
}  )  )  ;
return    JSON  .  stringify  (  tokens ,    null  ,    2  )    +    '\n'  ;
}  ,
}  )  ;


export    default    {
source  :    [  'tokens/**/*.json'  ]  ,
platforms  :    {
css  :    {
transformGroup  :    'css'  ,
buildPath  :    'build/css/'  ,
files  :    [  {
destination  :    'variables.css'  ,
format  :    'css/variables'  ,
options  :    {    outputReferences  :    true    }  ,
}  ]  ,
}  ,
agent  :    {
transformGroup  :    'css'  ,
buildPath  :    'build/agent/'  ,
files  :    [  {    destination  :    'tokens.json'  ,    format  :    'json/agent'    }  ]  ,
}  ,
}  ,
}  ;


```


The format reads two versions of each token.` token.$value` is the resolved value after transforms.` token.original.$value` is what you wrote, which is how the format knows` color.action.primary` was an alias of` color.blue.600` .


## Step 3: run the build


```text
npx style-dictionary build  --config   config.mjs


```


The real output:


```text
agent
✔︎ build /  agent /  tokens .  json


css
✔︎ build /  css /  variables .  css


```


### The CSS output, for the browser


```text
/**
* Do not edit directly, this file was auto-generated.
*/


:root     {
--color-blue-600  :    #2563eb  ;
--color-red-600  :    #dc2626  ;
--spacing-200  :    8  px  ;    /** Gap between an icon and its label. */
--spacing-400  :    16  px  ;    /** Padding inside buttons and inputs. */
--color-action-destructive  :    var  (  --color-red-600  )  ;    /** Background of actions that delete data. Pair with a confirm step. */
}


```


This is good CSS.` outputReferences: true` keeps the alias as a` var()` , so changing` --color-blue-600` updates every token that points at it. Descriptions come through as comments, which we didn't expect.


