Same six tokens, same source file, one build command.


## What the second file changes for an agent


Put the two outputs side by side and ask what an agent needs when it's about to style a Delete button:


Question the agent has From the CSS file From the agent JSON


Which token is for destructive actions? Guess from the variable name Search` description` for "delete"


What colour is it? Follow` var(--color-red-600)` to another line` value` is` #dc2626`


Is it a semantic token or a primitive? Infer from the naming pattern` aliasOf` is set, so it's semantic


Any rule attached? Parse the comment` description` : "Pair with a confirm step."


What do I write in the code? The variable name` cssVar` is` --color-action-destructive`


The CSS is **readable** : a person scanning it gets everything. The JSON is **resolvable** : every answer is a field, so an agent can look one token up by name and get a definite answer instead of reconstructing it. That's the gap between documentation written for people and context built for agents, and it shows up even in a six-token file. We go further into it in[what an agentic design system is](https://www.supernova.io/blog/what-is-an-agentic-design-system) .


A file like this is also what a token tool on a[design system MCP server](https://www.supernova.io/blog/design-system-mcp-servers) returns. If you serve tokens to agents over MCP, building this JSON in the same Style Dictionary run means the server and the CSS can't drift apart.


## What we'd add before using it for real


The example is small on purpose. On a real system, add these before you point an agent at it:


- **Themes and modes:** build one agent file per theme, or add a` modes` field. The stable spec describes modes in a separate[resolver file](https://github.com/design-tokens/community-group/blob/main/technical-reports/resolver/introduction.md) .
- **Descriptions on semantic tokens only:** primitives like` color.blue.600` don't need one. Every semantic token does, or the agent is back to guessing from names.
- **A check in CI:** fail the build when a semantic token has no` $description` . A custom format or a short script over` dictionary.allTokens` can do it.
- **Deprecation:** DTCG has a` $deprecated` property. Pass it through, so an agent stops using a token before you delete it.


If you're building the rest of a system around this file, our guide on[how to build a design system with AI](https://www.supernova.io/blog/how-to-build-a-design-system-with-ai) covers where tokens fit in the first week.


## Where Supernova fits


Style Dictionary is a good build step. It doesn't hold the descriptions, usage rules and component guidance that make the agent file worth reading, and it doesn't serve them.[Supernova](https://www.supernova.io/design-system-management) keeps tokens with their documentation, runs code export pipelines from them, and serves the same tokens to agents through its MCP server, with unlimited lookups on every plan including Free.


## Run it yourself in ten minutes


Make an empty folder, run` npm install style-dictionary@5.7.1` , and paste the token file and config from this post. Then ask your coding agent which token to use for a destructive action, once with the CSS file in context and once with the JSON. Compare how it gets to the answer.
